{
  "id": 219609631,
  "iid": 10441,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "Fix SSHv2 decryption support after closing and reopening a capture file",
  "description": "This PR fixes the faulty behaviour of the SSH keylog file handling within the SSH packet dissector. As of now, the dissector initializes the hash table containing the cryptographic keys within `proto_register_ssh`. The hash table is then populated with entries from the configured keylog file in `ssh_keylog_hash_write_secret()` by calling `ssh_keylog_read_file()`.\n\nHowever, all entries of the hash table are file scoped thus being destroyed when closing the capture file. This is not true for the hash table and the file pointer itself which maintain their state across different capture files and are only free'd on program exit. As the file pointer is not being reset, `ssh_keylog_read_file()` will not re-read the keylog file in subsequent calls - even across different capture files. As a consequence, decryption breaks (since keys and entries of the hash table have been free'd) whenever a SSH capture file is being reopened.\n\nI added a proper `ssh_init` and `ssh_cleanup` method to the dissector and registered them with Wireshark. `ssh_init` will create a new hash table and populate it via `ssh_keylog_read_file()` while `ssh_cleanup` will properly destroy the very same table as well as the file pointer. As a consequence, `ssh_keylog_read_file()` has been removed from `ssh_keylog_hash_write_secret()`. The overall concept has been adapted from the TLS packet dissector.",
  "state": "merged",
