    {
      "id": "c67290dc47cdd6374142711f00e1be9599b09d13",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1366628676,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Right now this ssh code is based on what's in tls and wireguard (so @Lekensteyn may have comments).\n\nThe implementation uses a `g_hash_table()` so that the keylog file is read and processed only when needed, and if and only if it has changed since the last time it was read. It is less efficient to process it once per file loading, as that means unnecessary extra file reads when a file is reopened - even if a capture file does not have any ssh, as all init routines are called - and that also means that files have to be reloaded if the file changes.\n\nIt's true that the entries of the hash table should not be file scoped if a `g_hash_table()` is used. `ssh_kex_make_bignum` always creates file scoped entries. They could (as with wireguard or tls) be copied to glib allocated members, and freed when the hash table is destroyed by creating the hash table with `g_hash_table_new_full()` appropriately.\n\nWith wireguard and TLS, there is also a UAT, so there's a shutdown routine for the UAT that destroys the keys. Without a UAT, you would call `register_shutdown_routine()` instead of `register_cleanup_routine()` to ensure that the hash table is freed at exit (and possibly also call `g_hash_table_remove_all()` when resetting the key log file when it has changed, to evict the old copies - wireguard doesn't do that when just the keylog file changes, only when the UAT does; I'm not sure if that's intentional.)\n\nAnother option is to copy the file scope entries to epan scoped entries, and use a `wmem_map_t` that is epan scoped instead of a `g_hash_table`. That would use more memory if the map was cleared when the keylog file was changed (unless the values are explicitly `wmem_free()`d upon removal), but might be simpler to manage the memory in another sense.\n\nLast option is to make the map a `wmem_map_t` that is autoreset epan scope / file scope, and just deal with the keylog file only being read once at startup, but reading an external file each time a new file is loaded even if it doesn't have ssh is a bit inefficient, as mentioned.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
